        "level": "Intermediate",
        "category": "System Design & Scalability",
        "question": "Compare Docker containerization with traditional Virtual Machines (VMs).",
        "answer": "Virtual Machines virtualize the physical hardware layer and run a complete guest operating system with its own kernel, resulting in large image sizes (several gigabytes) and slow boot times (minutes). Docker containers virtualize at the operating system level, sharing the host OS kernel and isolating processes using Linux cgroups and namespaces. Containers are lightweight (tens of megabytes), start in milliseconds, and utilize host memory far more efficiently.",
        "keyTakeaways": [
          "Understands OS kernel sharing vs guest OS virtualization",
          "Explains Linux namespaces and cgroups process isolation",
          "Identifies performance, density, and startup time differences"
        ]
