        "category": "Production Debugging & Observability",
        "question": "How do you design an idempotent API endpoint?",
        "answer": "An idempotent endpoint produces the exact same server state regardless of whether it is called once or multiple times with the same parameters (e.g., PUT, DELETE, or specialized POST). For payment or order creation, idempotency is achieved by having the client provide a unique Idempotency-Key header. The server checks a cache or database with a unique constraint: if the key exists, it returns the cached previous result; if not, it executes the operation and stores the result under that key.",
        "keyTakeaways": [
          "Defines mathematical idempotency in distributed APIs",
          "Explains Idempotency-Key header pattern",
          "Prevents duplicate billing or duplicate record creations"
        ]
