        "category": "Data Management & Relational Modeling",
        "question": "What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous operations in this domain?",
        "answer": "Synchronous operations block the calling thread until the requested computation or I/O operation completes and returns a response, making them simple but prone to thread starvation under high concurrency. Asynchronous operations return execution control immediately using event loops, promises, or callback queues, allowing the runtime to handle other operations while waiting for I/O, which dramatically boosts throughput.",
        "keyTakeaways": [
          "Explains thread blocking vs non-blocking I/O",
          "Describes event loops and asynchronous primitives",
          "Identifies when to choose sync vs async"
        ]
