        "question": "What are the primary differences between SQL (Relational) and NoSQL databases?",
        "answer": "SQL databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL) are relational, schema-enforced, and ACID-compliant, making them ideal for structured financial and transactional records where consistency is paramount. NoSQL databases (MongoDB, DynamoDB, Cassandra) offer flexible schemas (document, key-value, column-family) and horizontal scaling, suitable for high-velocity unstructured telemetry and catalog data where availability is prioritized over strict consistency.",
